Subject: [PATCH] dxmt: another queue's GPU waits for the flushed writes of a shared texture (cranvr)

Direct3D 11 asks a writer to call Flush before another device uses a resource that they share without a keyed mutex.
After that the reader sees the writes. Metal orders no work of two command queues on a texture. DXMT made the
reader's queue wait for nothing. A second process could copy a texture of the stock path (MTLSharedTextureHandle) or
an IOSurface texture of patch 0019 before the writer's GPU had run the clear that the writer flushed before it sent
the handle (cranvr issue #177). A keyed mutex orders its texture already (patch 0014). Such a texture takes no part.

- dxmt_shared_order.hpp and .cpp: each queue that holds a shared texture serves its shared event and its resource
  initializer's event through winemetal's broker (patch 0020). A section of shared memory has a slot for each queue
  that holds the texture. The texture's unique name names the section. A slot holds the event value of the queue's
  newest chunk that wrote the texture and the value of the write before it. The creator's slot also holds the
  initializer's value of the initial contents.
- The queue's encode thread notes the shared textures of a chunk in ArgumentEncodingContext::access. After the
  chunk's commands ran and before its first encoder, the order step stores the chunk's writes in its slots. It reads
  the slots of the other queues. It encodes a wait on another queue's event only when the other GPU has not reached
  the value. No CPU waits for a GPU.
- A commit takes a stamp from one counter of all DXMT processes of the session. A reader waits only for chunks that
  the writer committed before the reader's chunk. When such a chunk has not passed its order step yet, the reader's
  encode thread waits for the writer's encode thread (at most 100 ms). The GPU waits only for chunks that wrote the
  texture and passed their order step. So waits point back in one order: two processes that read each other's
  textures cannot wait for each other.
- The order state lives in the TextureAllocation: every chunk that used it keeps it until the GPU has finished that
  chunk. The Texture itself may go earlier, during the encode.
- The creator submits a shared texture's initial contents at once. A reader waits for that work too.
- A watch thread sets the events of a process that ended to the values that a GPU of this process waits for. A
  destroyed queue marks its section closed.
- Each queue logs its counts every 10 s and at its end: the command buffers with shared textures, the waits that it
  encoded, the time of the step and the waits for another encode thread. DXMT_SHARED_ORDER=0 turns the order off.
  DXMT_SHARED_ORDER_STATS=1 also times each encoded wait until the other GPU's signal.

